WEBVTT

00:00:00.254 --> 00:00:04.845
NIST's post-quantum cryptography effort used a multi-year public process to evaluate

00:00:04.845 --> 00:00:05.970
candidate algorithms.

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:08.945
That process included security analysis, performance review,

00:00:08.945 --> 00:00:12.225
implementation considerations, and public cryptographic review.

00:00:12.225 --> 00:00:16.245
The open process matters because cryptographic confidence grows slowly.

00:00:16.245 --> 00:00:19.930
Algorithms need to survive review, attempted attacks, implementation experiments,

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:24.265
and scrutiny from different communities before they are suitable for broad reliance.

00:00:24.265 --> 00:00:26.645
Security review is only one part of the evaluation.

00:00:26.645 --> 00:00:30.800
Algorithms also need acceptable performance, implementability, clear parameter choices,

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:32.880
and behavior that can fit into real protocols.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:37.310
A strong algorithm on paper can still create deployment problems if keys are too large for

00:00:37.310 --> 00:00:38.535
constrained environments,

00:00:38.535 --> 00:00:40.600
signatures are too slow for common use cases,

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:44.525
or implementations are difficult to make correct and resistant to side channels.

00:00:44.525 --> 00:00:47.041
The words selected and standardized are not interchangeable.

00:00:47.041 --> 00:00:51.240
A selected algorithm has been chosen for a path toward a standard, but the final document,

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:54.920
naming, parameters, and implementation guidance may still be in progress.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:58.640
A standardized algorithm has a published standard that organizations and vendors can

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:00.245
reference more directly.

00:01:00.245 --> 00:01:04.035
Migration programs should track both statuses so planning does not freeze at a single

00:01:04.035 --> 00:01:05.170
announcement.

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:07.115
Organizations should also expect guidance to evolve.

00:01:07.115 --> 00:01:10.685
Standards, drafts, validation programs, protocol profiles, product support,

00:01:10.685 --> 00:01:12.985
and compliance expectations mature over time.

00:01:12.985 --> 00:01:17.920
A good PQC program watches the standards process, but it does not wait passively.

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:21.670
It builds inventory, tests dependencies, asks vendors concrete questions,

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:25.120
and designs cryptographic agility so future changes are manageable.
